WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:10.480
The single most stunning economic story of the&nbsp;
last few decades has been the rise of China.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:17.440
From 1980 to 2020, China’s economy grew more&nbsp;
than 75-fold. Huge new cities were built.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:21.360
Hundreds of millions escaped&nbsp;
poverty. It was the largest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:25.840
and most rapid improvement in material&nbsp;
conditions on record in modern history.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:31.760
Let’s go back. When the economist&nbsp;
Adam Smith was writing in the 1700s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:37.840
he considered China to be one of the wealthiest&nbsp;
countries that had ever existed. But after decades&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:43.840
of war and instability in the 19th and early&nbsp;
20th century, China began a rapid decline.

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:49.280
Up until a few decades ago, China was&nbsp;
one of the poorest countries on earth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:51.680
But now China is an economic powerhouse.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:59.200
Economists predict that it will overtake the US as&nbsp;
the largest economy in the world in this decade.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:04.880
People called it “the Chinese miracle.”
 
You can hear some people describe this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:10.640
“miracle” as a straightforward story of the&nbsp;
free market. They say it’s a simple story:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:15.920
“China was poor. Then the economy was freed&nbsp;
from the grip of the state. Now China is rich.”

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:23.840
But this is misleading. China’s rise was NOT about&nbsp;
the triumph of the free market. To understand why,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:28.800
we have to look at what happened to other&nbsp;
countries which remade their own economies in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:35.520
the same period, often to disastrous effects.
 
Since the 1980s, free market policies have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:40.800
swept the globe. Many countries have&nbsp;
undergone far-ranging transformations:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:47.040
liberalizing all prices, privatizing entire&nbsp;
industries, and opening up to free trade.

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:54.080
But many of the economies that were subjected to&nbsp;
markets overnight have since stagnated or decayed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:59.760
NONE have had a growth record anything like&nbsp;
the one seen in China. African countries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:06.080
endured brutal economic shrinkage. Latin America&nbsp;
experienced twenty-five years of stagnation.
&nbsp;

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:10.960
If we compare China to Russia, the other&nbsp;
giant of Communism in the 20th century,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:15.040
the contrast is even more&nbsp;
staggering. Under state socialism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:20.560
Russia was an industrial superpower, while&nbsp;
China was still largely an agricultural economy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:26.320
Yet during the same period that Chinese&nbsp;
reform led it to incredible economic growth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:33.520
Russia’s reform led it to a brutal collapse.
 
Both China and Russia had been economies that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:39.840
were organized largely through state commands.&nbsp;
Individual players could only act within the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:46.080
structures set by state planning. Think of&nbsp;
playing foosball. Each individual player&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:51.840
can only be moved along with the rod to&nbsp;
which it is attached. It is a rigid set-up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:58.080
you can only go back and forth or rotate.
 
Market reforms in both Russia and China was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:04.320
like moving from foosball to an actual soccer&nbsp;
game. Players now could move freely. But while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:10.320
Russia jumped right into the game without&nbsp;
setting up a proper stadium, rules, or jury,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:16.480
in China the state took the lead in setting up&nbsp;
all infrastructure. The state built the team,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:21.280
it trained the players, educated the&nbsp;
coaches and designed an overall strategy.
&nbsp;

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:25.520
Russia followed the recommendations&nbsp;
of the most quote-unquote scientific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:32.240
economics at the time, a policy of so-called&nbsp;
“shock therapy.” As a basic principle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:38.240
the idea was that the old planned economy had&nbsp;
to be destroyed to make space for the market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:46.080
to emerge. Think of shock therapy like knocking&nbsp;
over a Jenga tower. After a short period of pain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:51.360
Russia was supposed to emerge as a fully-fledged&nbsp;
capitalist economy, almost overnight.

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:57.840
The leader of the plan announced it was “a way&nbsp;
of destroying Communism in Russia.” When Russian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:04.240
president Boris Yeltsin took power, he eliminated&nbsp;
all price controls, privatized state-owned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:10.240
companies and assets, and immediately opened&nbsp;
Russia up to global trade. So, what happened?

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:19.280
In a word: catastrophe.
 
Shock therapy was a fatal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:24.240
blow to the Russian economy. The shock&nbsp;
therapists had predicted some short-term pain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:31.200
but what they didn’t see coming was how severe,&nbsp;
destructive, and permanent the effects would be.

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:37.200
Consumer prices spiraled out of control.&nbsp;
Hyperinflation took hold. Government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:45.040
coffers were looted. GDP fell by 40 percent. The&nbsp;
“shock therapy” recession in Russia was deeper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:49.920
and longer than the American Great&nbsp;
Depression by a large margin.

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:56.320
This was a disaster for ordinary Russian&nbsp;
people. HIV infections, alcoholism, childhood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.320 --> 00:05:03.200
malnutrition, and crime went through the roof.&nbsp;
Life expectancy for Russian men fell by SEVEN&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:11.680
YEARS, more than any industrialized country has&nbsp;
ever experienced in peacetime. In 2006, Russian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:18.640
life expectancy was still several years LOWER&nbsp;
than it had been in 1986, under the Soviet Union.

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:24.960
It turned out that Russia didn’t get&nbsp;
a successful “free market” overnight.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:31.760
Instead it went from a stagnating economy to a&nbsp;
hollowed-out wreckage dominated by oligarchs.

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:36.960
If simply getting rid of price&nbsp;
controls and government employment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:43.600
didn’t make a country prosper – and in fact,&nbsp;
destroyed its economy and killed huge numbers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:51.840
of people – then clearly the rapid application&nbsp;
of “free markets” was not the simple solution.

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:52.160
But what did China do differently? Let me explain.

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:56.640
When Deng Xiaoping took over&nbsp;
the leadership of China in 1978,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.640 --> 00:06:05.040
the country he inherited was STILL desperately&nbsp;
poor. In 1980, China had a per capita GDP of just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:12.320
one hundred and ninety-four dollars. That was less&nbsp;
than Sudan and Haiti – and almost HALF of Niger.

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:18.800
The Chinese leadership knew they needed&nbsp;
reform. As I show in my book “How China&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:25.440
Escaped Shock Therapy,” throughout the 1980s,&nbsp;
the Chinese leadership repeatedly considered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:32.880
implementing the same type of sudden reforms that&nbsp;
Russia pursued. The idea of starting from a clean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:39.120
slate seemed attractive, and shock therapy was&nbsp;
widely promoted by quote-unquote “scientific”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:45.200
economics. Programs for rapid price liberalization&nbsp;
were prepared and then withdrawn, twice.

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:50.240
But in the end, they decided not&nbsp;
to pursue shock therapy. Unlike the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:56.160
free-market economists in Russia, the Chinese&nbsp;
leadership approached change like a game of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.160 --> 00:07:04.880
Jenga. Take out many pieces at once and the whole&nbsp;
thing falls apart. Take out one piece at a time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:10.480
and you can win the whole game.

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:18.000
Instead of knocking over the Jenga tower, China&nbsp;
reformed itself in an experimental and gradual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:24.160
way. Market activities were tolerated or actively&nbsp;
promoted in non-essential parts of the economy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:31.040
China implemented a system of what they called&nbsp;
“dual-track pricing.” State-owned enterprises&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:35.920
and farmers had to deliver their quotas to&nbsp;
the government at a certain price set by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:42.240
government. But if they managed to produce more,&nbsp;
they could sell their surplus at market prices.

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:47.120
China was learning from the real story&nbsp;
of the world’s most developed nations:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:53.760
countries like the United States, Britain, Japan,&nbsp;
and South Korea. Each of these in their own way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:57.840
managed and planned the development&nbsp;
of their economies and markets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:01.440
protecting early-stage industries&nbsp;
and controlling investment.

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:07.040
Western free-market economists thought&nbsp;
this system would be a disaster. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:12.720
American economist Milton Friedman wrote an&nbsp;
open letter to Deng’s premier Zhao Ziyang.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:19.200
He said that the dual-price system was&nbsp;
a bad idea, and that China should “free&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:25.600
prices and wages…[in] one bold&nbsp;
stroke.” Just like in Russia.

00:08:25.600 --> 00:08:30.800
But China’s leaders didn’t listen. And while&nbsp;
Russia collapsed after following the “shock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:37.600
therapy” program, China saw remarkable success.&nbsp;
The state kept control over the backbone of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:44.080
industrial economy, as well as the ownership&nbsp;
over land. As China grew into the new dynamics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:50.800
of its economy, state institutions were not&nbsp;
degraded to fossils from the past but were often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:57.600
the drivers at the frontier of new industries,&nbsp;
protecting and guaranteeing their own growth.

00:08:58.160 --> 00:09:02.160
China today is not a free-market&nbsp;
economy in any sense of the word.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:08.240
It is a STATE-LED MARKET ECONOMY. The&nbsp;
government effectively owns all land,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:14.000
and China leverages state ownership through&nbsp;
market competition to steer the economy.

00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:20.400
The “shock therapy” approach advocated&nbsp;
around the world was a failure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.400 --> 00:09:23.760
While Russia collapsed after&nbsp;
its sudden transformation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:29.680
China’s gradual reforms allowed it to&nbsp;
thrive. And that made all the difference.

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:34.080
I’m Isabella Weber, assistant&nbsp;
professor of economics at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:49.840
the University of Massachusetts&nbsp;
Amherst, for the Gravel Institute.

